# OpenTelemetry

The nx-fhir `otel` feature gives this server OpenTelemetry route tagging and a bounded request counter. The OpenTelemetry Java agent records the HTTP server spans, metrics, and logs. The code below gives each FHIR request a bounded route and counts it on the `fhir.server.requests` metric.

When the agent is not attached, the OpenTelemetry API calls do nothing, and the request log query-string filter stays active.

## What the feature adds

The feature adds `FhirRouteInterceptor` and `TelemetryProperties` under `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/telemetry/`, `TelemetryConfig` under `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/`, the unit tests `FhirRouteInterceptorTest` and `TelemetryConfigTest` under `src/test/java/<%= packagePath %>/`, and the dependencies `io.opentelemetry.instrumentation:opentelemetry-instrumentation-api` and `io.opentelemetry:opentelemetry-api` to `pom.xml`, with the versions that the HAPI FHIR parent pom manages.

## Route templates

The interceptor sets `http.route` to one of these templates. `{type}` stands for any resource type, and `$name` for any operation that the server has registered at its first request.

| Route | Requests |
| --- | --- |
| `/fhir/metadata` | Capability statement |
| `/fhir` | Transaction and batch bundles |
| `/fhir/{type}` | Type-level search and create |
| `/fhir/{type}/{id}` | Read, update, patch, and delete |
| `/fhir/{type}/{id}/_history/{version}` | Version read |
| `/fhir/{type}/$name` | Type-level operation |
| `/fhir/{type}/{id}/$name` | Instance-level operation |
| `/fhir/$name` | System-level operation |
| `/fhir/{operation}`, `/fhir/{type}/{operation}`, `/fhir/{type}/{id}/{operation}` | An operation name the server did not register, which only a wildcard provider binds |
| `/fhir/unhandled` | A request HAPI rejected before it bound a handler, such as an unknown resource type |
| `/fhir/other` | A request an interceptor answered before HAPI bound a handler and did not name through `tagRoute` |

HAPI's own tokens `metadata`, `_search`, `_history`, and `$graphql` take the place of `$name`. Any other operation name, which is not a token and was not registered at the first request, becomes `{operation}`, also the name of an operation that a provider registers after the first request.

## Query strings and identifiers

No route and no metric attribute holds a resource id, a query string, or other client text. The span keeps the real path, with its ids, in `url.path`. By default, the interceptor overwrites the span attribute `url.query` of a FHIR request with `[redacted]`, and `TelemetryConfig` removes `${requestParameters}` from the request log format, `hapi.fhir.logger.format`, because search query strings can carry patient identifiers. To capture query strings, for example on a server that holds only synthetic data, set `telemetry.capture-query-strings: true` in `application.yaml` or the environment variable `TELEMETRY_CAPTURE_QUERY_STRINGS=true`, and restart the server.

## Request metric

The interceptor adds one to the counter `fhir.server.requests`, in Prometheus `fhir_server_requests_total`, for each FHIR request, also for a request that HAPI bound to no handler. Each attribute is a Prometheus label with `_` in place of `.`:

- `fhir.interaction`: `read`, `vread`, `search`, `create`, `update`, `patch`, `delete`, `history`, `operation`, `capabilities`, `batch`, `transaction`, `validate`, `meta`, `graphql`, `unhandled`, or `other`. These two mean the same as the routes `/fhir/unhandled` and `/fhir/other`, and `other` also counts a HAPI operation type with no value of its own.
- `fhir.resource_type`: a registered resource type, or `other`. It is absent for system-level and unhandled requests.
- `fhir.operation`: an operation name that a provider registered at the first request declares, such as `$everything`, or `other`. A wildcard provider's `$*` is not a name. It is absent unless the interaction is `operation`.

`telemetry.resource-types` in `application.yaml` (default `[]`) limits which registered resource types get their own value, and each other registered type counts as `other`. An empty list records each registered type. The setting changes only the metric label, and the server still serves each registered resource type. The registered types are each type of the FHIR version when `hapi.fhir.supported_resource_types` is empty, else that list plus `SearchParameter`.

The agent's SDK limits each instrument to `otel.java.metrics.cardinality.limit` attribute sets, 2,000 by default. Past the limit, it records each new combination on one overflow series, which has the label `otel_metric_overflow="true"`. On a public server, set `telemetry.resource-types` so that the metric stays well below the limit.

### Other metrics

- `fhir.server.errors`, in Prometheus `fhir_server_errors_total`: one for each FHIR request with a response status of 400 or more, with `fhir.interaction` and `fhir.outcome` (`client_error` for 400 to 499, `server_error` for 500 or more), and no resource type, operation, or status code.
- `fhir.server.start_time`, in Prometheus `fhir_server_start_time_seconds`: the start time of the JVM in seconds since the Unix epoch, with no attributes.

## Agent settings

The agent reads a setting from a system property first, then from an environment variable, then from the file that `OTEL_JAVAAGENT_CONFIGURATION_FILE` names. A variable you set wins over the file.

The agent settings of this feature are `OTEL_INSTRUMENTATION_COMMON_DEFAULT_ENABLED=false` and `OTEL_INSTRUMENTATION_<NAME>_ENABLED=true` for only these instrumentations: `TOMCAT` (the server span and the `http.server.request.duration` histogram), `SERVLET`, `OPENTELEMETRY_API`, `OPENTELEMETRY_INSTRUMENTATION_API`, `LOGBACK_APPENDER` (log records), and `RUNTIME_TELEMETRY` (the `jvm.*` metrics). Without `OPENTELEMETRY_INSTRUMENTATION_API`, every route stays at the servlet mapping `/fhir/*`.
<% if (compose || serve) { -%>
`otel/agent.properties` holds these settings and the service name.
<% } -%>

| Variable | Value | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `OTEL_SERVICE_NAME` | `<%= serviceName %>` | The `service.name` resource attribute. |
| `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` | Agent default `http://localhost:4318` | Where the agent sends OTLP over HTTP. |
| `OTEL_JAVAAGENT_CONFIGURATION_FILE` | `otel/agent.properties` | The properties file the agent reads. |
| `JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS` | `-javaagent:/app/opentelemetry-javaagent.jar` | Attaches the agent in the container image. |
