## Grafana dashboard

`otel/grafana/dashboards/<%= serviceName %>.json` is a Grafana v2 dashboard, and `otel/grafana/provisioning/dashboards/<%= serviceName %>.yaml` provisions it into a Grafana folder named `<%= serviceName %>`. The rows and their panels:

- Summary: Resource interactions, selected range (the requests with an interaction other than `operation`, `unhandled`, and `other`), Operation invocations, selected range, Last server start, and Server starts, selected range.
- Traffic: Resource interactions and Operations (the ten values of `fhir_resource_type` and of `fhir_operation` with the most requests in the selected range, `other` included), Requests per minute by interaction, Error share by interaction, Top requests (requests in range), Average processing time by route (traces), Unhandled request paths (traces), Average requests per minute, selected range, and Minutes since last metric sample.
- Runtime: CPU usage and Memory usage of the JVM.

The two trace panels read Tempo with TraceQL metrics queries, which need the metrics-generator with the local-blocks processor. `grafana/otel-lgtm` and Grafana Cloud have it on. "Unhandled request paths (traces)" shows client text and resource ids from the request path.

A change to the dashboard file overwrites the edits made in the Grafana UI when Grafana loads the file again. To keep a UI edit, export the dashboard JSON from Grafana and save it over the file.
<% } -%>

## Series budget

- Request counter: one series for each combination of label values that a running JVM has served. About 87 for 20 resource types with 4 interactions each, 3 operations, and 4 system rows, about 200 for a client that sends every interaction to every type with 10 types in `telemetry.resource-types`, and the SDK cap of 2,000 with no list on R4.
- Error counter: at most 34. Start time gauge: one for each running JVM.
- `jvm_*` metrics: 44 series measured on a HAPI server one minute after start on JDK 25 with the G1 collector, and about 55 at most, for each running JVM. The dashboard reads only `jvm_cpu_recent_utilization_ratio`, `jvm_memory_used_bytes`, and `jvm_memory_limit_bytes`.
- Three `io_hapifhir_validation_support_chain_*` gauges for each running instance.
- The `http.server.request.duration` histogram: 17 series for each route, method, and status code combination, which is about 200 for search and read traffic, 400 to 700 for mixed traffic, and up to about 2,500. No dashboard panel reads it.
- After a restart, the series of the old and the new JVM both count until the old ones pass the idle timeout of the backend.

A backend that counts series can drop the `jvm_*` metrics that the dashboard does not read with a drop rule of its own. To keep the histogram out of a billed backend, drop it on the way:

- In an OpenTelemetry Collector, a `filter` processor that drops the metric `http.server.request.duration`.
- On Grafana Cloud, an Adaptive Metrics drop rule for `http_server_request_duration_seconds_bucket`, `http_server_request_duration_seconds_sum`, and `http_server_request_duration_seconds_count`.
<% if (compose) { -%>
- In the local stack, uncomment these lines of the `otel-lgtm` service in `docker-compose.otel.yml`: `environment:`, the `OTELCOL_EXTRA_ARGS` line, <% if (!dashboard) { %>`volumes:`, <% } %>and the `otelcol-filter.yaml` volume line. Then recreate the `otel-lgtm` container. `otel/otelcol-filter.yaml` holds the filter, which drops only `http.server.request.duration`.
